  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Chadds Ford sits where Route 1 (Baltimore Pike) meets the Brandywine River, the historic crossing that gave the village its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://chaddsfordhistory.org/history/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1777, George Washington chose the Chadds Ford bridge as a defensive line during the Battle of Brandywine, halting the British advance toward Philadelphia.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://chaddsfordhistory.org/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Brandywine School artist colony, home to N.C. Wyeth and Howard Pyle, made Chadds Ford a hub for American illustration in the late 19th and early 20th centuries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chadds_Ford%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first railroad to reach Chadds Ford arrived in the late 1850s, linking the village to Wilmington and Philadelphia and boosting its agricultural economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://chaddsfordhistory.org/history/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 1800s, waterpower from the Brandywine drove mills that produced gunpowder, paper, grain, and timber, fueling regional growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://chaddsfordhistory.org/history/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The village’s historic district, listed on the National Register of Historic Places, preserves 19th‑century homes and the ferry landing that once carried John Chads’ passengers across the river.",
